<title>UK: Enlarged Boeing takes over top defence slot.</title>
<headline>Enlarged Boeing takes over top defence slot.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-12-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Boeing's planned takeover of McDonnell Douglas will allow it to leapfrog Lockheed Martin and become the world's largest defence and aerospace group in terms of sales.</p>
<p>Industry magazine Flight International business editor Kevin O'Toole told Reuters he estimated 1996 revenues, including civil aerospace, of the enlarged Boeing Co would be $38.5 billion against $27 billion for Lockheed.  </p>
<p>He has compiled Flight International's list of top 10 defence companies in the world ranked by estimated sales for 1996 and based on the magazine's &quot;Aerospace Top-100&quot; statistics for the first half of the year.</p>
<p>Figures include Boeing's recent purchases from Rockwell and Lockheed's buys from Loral, as well as completion of other mergers in the pipeline, including France's proposed merger of Aerospatiale and Dassault.  </p>
<p>    COMPANY				 ESTIMATED SALES</p>
<p>						(billions)</p>
<p> 1) McDonnell Douglas/Boeing	     $38.5</p>
<p> 2) Lockheed				   $27.0</p>
<p> 4) Hughes Electronics			$9.8</p>
<p> 5) British Aerospace			 $9.0</p>
<p> 6) Northrop Grumman			 $8.6</p>
<p> 7) United Technologies		    $8.5</p>
<p> 8) Raytheon				   $8.0</p>
<p> 9) Daimler Benz Aerospace		$7.5</p>
<p> 10) GE Aircraft and Engines		$6.7</p>
<p>RANKING BY DEFENCE AND SPACE REVENUES ONLY</p>
<p> 1) McDonnell Douglas/Boeing			 $19.0</p>
<p> 2) Lockheed Martin				    $18.0</p>
<p> 3) British Aerospace				  $7.0</p>
<p> 4) Northrop Grumman				   $6.5</p>
<p> 5) Hughes Electronics				 $6.0</p>
<p>-- London Newsroom ++ 44 171 542 7719</p>
</text>
